---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { line } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const changed = assertion('the-county-changed-sides')

const turned = assertion('the-county-did-not-turn-with-1896')

// The Democratic share at the eight presidential elections the 1885 county history records. From
// the assertion's own figures, so the chart cannot drift from the prose the gate checked.
const oldSpec = line(changed.figures.map((f) => f.label), [
    { name: 'Democratic share of the presidential vote', y: changed.figures.map((f) => f.value) },
  ], {
  yTitle: 'Per cent',
  tickSuffix: '%',
  labelLast: true,
})

// The 1884 count as the book prints it, twenty units and two columns. Typed out because the third
// and fourth columns are computed and the graph carries the pair the book printed.
const eighteenEightyFour = [
  ['Lima, First Ward', '364', '316', 'R+48'],
  ['Lima, Second Ward', '429', '282', 'R+147'],
  ['Lima, Third Ward', '103', '381', 'D+278'],
  ['Lima, Fourth Ward', '260', '139', 'R+121'],
  ['Auglaize', '279', '177', 'R+102'],
  ['Monroe', '267', '222', 'R+45'],
  ['Sugar Creek', '133', '111', 'R+22'],
  ['Ottawa', '15', '18', 'D+3'],
  ['Perry', '155', '190', 'D+35'],
  ['Shawnee', '117', '171', 'D+54'],
  ['Bath', '129', '192', 'D+63'],
  ['Delphos, First Ward', '89', '163', 'D+74'],
  ['Marion', '211', '302', 'D+91'],
  ['Richland — Beaver Dam', '85', '176', 'D+91'],
  ['Amanda', '100', '207', 'D+107'],
  ['Jackson', '159', '273', 'D+114'],
  ['Delphos, Second Ward', '85', '200', 'D+115'],
  ['German', '108', '250', 'D+142'],
  ['Spencer', '159', '322', 'D+163'],
  ['Richland — Bluffton', '125', '349', 'D+224'],
  ['Allen County, as printed', '3,372', '4,442', 'D+1,070'],
]

// The Democratic margin as a share of the vote cast, 1884 to 1908. Signed: the 1904 term is
// negative because the Republican carried that one, so the line dips through zero where the county
// changed hands and comes back. From the assertion's own figures.
const marginSpec = line(turned.figures.map((f) => f.label), [
    { name: 'Democratic margin, points of the vote cast', y: turned.figures.map((f) => f.value) },
  ], {
  yTitle: 'Points',
  labelLast: true,
})

// The six elections as the Secretary of State printed them, with the margin computed. Typed out
// because the corpus holds them as one measure and the site wants the rows.
const sixElections = [
  ['1888', 'Harrison', '3,761', 'Cleveland', '5,123', '274', '9,158', 'D+1,362'],
  ['1892', 'Harrison', '3,579', 'Cleveland', '4,945', '902', '9,426', 'D+1,366'],
  ['1896', 'McKinley', '4,959', 'Bryan', '6,360', '210', '11,529', 'D+1,401'],
  ['1900', 'McKinley', '5,281', 'Bryan', '6,540', '200', '12,021', 'D+1,259'],
  ['1904', 'Roosevelt', '6,091', 'Parker', '4,980', '532', '11,603', 'R+1,111'],
  ['1908', 'Taft', '5,841', 'Bryan', '7,195', '682', '13,718', 'D+1,354'],
]
---

<Article slug="the-county-changed-sides">
  <p>
    Everything above is three elections wide. The county's own history is not: its seventh chapter
    is an election-returns appendix that prints the vote office by office and year by year from
    1831 to 1884, and nobody had read it here. Read for president, it says that the county now
    voting 69–29 Republican was Democratic ground for most of the nineteenth century.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={changed} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="democratic-share-1856-1884"
    title="Democratic share of Allen County's presidential vote, 1856 to 1884"
    caption="Seven of eight go Democratic. The exception is 1864, and 1864 is the one year the book prints its two figures marked (estimated) — the only round numbers in the chapter."
    columns={['Election', 'Democratic share']}
    rows={changed.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value}%`])}
    height={300}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="democratic-share-1856-1884" set:html={JSON.stringify(oldSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The 1884 count is printed by township and ward, and it is the last sub-county return this site
    holds before 2016. Lima's four wards went Republican 1,156 to 1,118 while the county went
    Democratic 3,372 to 4,442 — the city was the Republican end of Allen County and the townships
    the Democratic end, which is the arrangement of 2020 turned end for end. The city was already
    split by ward, too: its Second was 60 per cent Republican and its Third 79 per cent Democratic,
    a mile apart.
  </p>
  <DataTable
    title="Allen County by township and ward, 4 November 1884"
    caption="Sorted by margin, Republican units first. Ottawa Township polls thirty-three votes — a seventh of the next smallest unit — twenty-seven years after it was erected to hold a growing Lima. German Township, which the county renamed American in 1918, polls 358."
    columns={['Unit', 'Rep.', 'Dem.', 'Margin']}
    rows={eighteenEightyFour}
  />
  <p class="note">
    The Republican column adds to its printed total of 3,372 exactly. The Democratic column adds to
    4,441 against a printed 4,442, and this site does not know whether that vote is the book's
    arithmetic or its typesetting; the county figure carried forward is the book's own. Getting
    even that far meant discarding both OCR passes of the page: each renders Lima's Third Ward as
    108 Republican where the page says 103, and those five votes are exactly what reconciles the
    column.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then a gap of thirty-two years, and one figure inside it. The Ohio Secretary of State printed
    his own certified abstract of the 1920 returns, county by county, and Allen County went to
    Warren G. Harding 13,978 to James M. Cox's 11,658, with 429 for Eugene V. Debs — a Republican
    plurality of 2,320 on 26,082 votes. It is the first official state election return this site
    holds; every other election figure here comes from a county history or from a volunteer
    transcription of documents the Secretary of State's present-day site will not serve to an
    automated client. The office that returns 403 in 2026 answered in 1921, in a book.
  </p>

  <h2>It did not turn with 1896</h2>
  <p>
    That gap is now six-eighths closed, and the office that closed it is the same one. The
    Secretary of State's returns for these elections were not missing; they were filed under
    another title. Three of the four <em>Ohio Election Statistics</em> items on archive.org are
    bound runs of several years rather than single volumes, and before that title existed the
    returns were a division of his <em>Annual Report</em>. Six volumes, six elections, and four of
    them read out of reports published two, four or twelve years after the vote — because the
    office reprints a county's presidential row beside the next odd year's governor.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={turned} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="democratic-margin-1884-1908"
    title="Democratic margin in Allen County, points of the vote cast"
    caption="Signed, so 1904 is below the line: the county went Republican once and came straight back. The line falls steadily and the count behind it does not — every one of these seven margins is between 1,070 and 1,401 votes."
    columns={['Election', 'Democratic margin']}
    rows={turned.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value} pts`])}
    height={300}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="democratic-margin-1884-1908" set:html={JSON.stringify(marginSpec)} is:inline />
  <DataTable
    title="Allen County for president, 1888 to 1908"
    caption="Bryan is on the 1896 ballot twice, Democratic and People's; the 34 votes on his second line are counted in the others column here, and moving them across takes his share from 55.2 to 55.5. The 1892 others column — 902 votes, 9.6 per cent — is half again the state's rate in the same table."
    columns={['Year', 'Republican', 'Votes', 'Democrat', 'Votes', 'Others', 'Total', 'Margin']}
    rows={sixElections}
  />
  <p class="note">
    One of these figures is printed two ways by the office that certified it. McKinley's 1896 vote
    in Allen County is 4,959 in the abstract for that election and in the comparative table of the
    1904 volume, and 4,956 in the comparative table of the 1908 volume. Nothing inside the 1908
    volume could catch it: its comparative tables print two columns a year and no totals, no
    pluralities and no state row, so 4,956 breaks no identity on its own page. Only another
    printing could, and the corpus now prefers the abstract and publishes the divergence.
  </p>
  <p>
    What is left is 1912 and 1916. Archive.org holds no Ohio volume covering either, and those two
    elections are where a margin that had sat between 1,070 and 1,401 votes for a quarter of a
    century became Harding's 2,320.
  </p>
</Article>
